    <h2 id="why-the-numbers-can-be-trusted">Gold and versions</h2>
    <p>
      v1 graded the model on 300 snippets it generated itself.
      <code>industry</code> recall was 0.217. v2 replaced that set with 54
      hand-labeled real snippets from the DoD news wire and SEC filings. An Opus
      <Term id="judge-model">judge</Term> cross-checked them. On that gold,
      <code>industry</code> <Term id="f1">F1</Term> went to 1.000.
    </p>

    <table class="metrics">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Metric</th><th>v1 synthetic</th><th>v2 real (Sonnet 4.6)</th><th>v2 + Sonnet 5</th><th>v2 + prompt fix</th><th>v3 three-axis (current, v3.2.1)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Category accuracy</td><td>79.0%</td><td>88.9%</td><td>88.9%</td><td>94.4%</td><td><span data-metric="category_accuracy">94.4%</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Category macro-F1</td><td>0.765</td><td>0.906</td><td>0.888</td><td>0.950</td><td><span data-metric="category_macro_f1">0.930</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Domain accuracy</td><td>97.3%</td><td>88.9%</td><td>94.4%</td><td>92.6%</td><td><span data-metric="domain_accuracy">98.1%</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Domain macro-F1</td><td>0.973</td><td>0.894</td><td>0.947</td><td>0.932</td><td><span data-metric="domain_macro_f1">0.982</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Region accuracy</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><span data-metric="region_accuracy">94.4%</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Region macro-F1</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><span data-metric="region_macro_f1">0.975</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Every column after the first is the same 54 hand-labeled snippets. In July
      2026 the workhorse moved from Sonnet 4.6 to Sonnet 5 on that set. Domain
      rose to 94.4%. Category did not move.
    </p>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">The ceiling that wasn&rsquo;t</p>
      <p>A per-cell confusion breakdown showed that the remaining misses were
        not scattered. They clustered on one boundary. The model read
        <code>technology</code> as <code>operations</code> whenever a new system
        was tested or demoed in an operational setting. It also over-assigned
        <code>land</code>. Two targeted rubric clauses moved category from 90.7%
        to <strong>94.4%</strong> and domain from 90.7% to 92.6%.
        <code>technology</code> recall reached 1.000, and nothing regressed.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The lesson:</strong> the misses were a prompt
        problem. A stronger model did not move that boundary.</p>
    </div>


    <div class="toc-return"><a href="#toc-heading">Contents</a></div>
    <h2 id="what-the-eval-caught">What the eval found</h2>

    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>The prompt that read better and performed worse</p>
      <p>I sharpened the system prompt to separate <code>procurement</code> from
        <code>industry</code>. <code>procurement</code> is a purchase from the
        buyer's view, and <code>industry</code> is a company's own business news.
        Category accuracy fell from 79.0% to 76.7%, and <code>industry</code>
        recall dropped from 0.217 to 0.100. The sharper rule gave the model a
        cleaner excuse to move borderline company stories into
        <code>procurement</code>. I reverted it.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The lesson:</strong> the prompt read cleaner.
        The numbers did not. Reverted.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision" id="decision-grounding" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>The grounding that did not pay</p>
      <p>I added <Term id="bm25">BM25</Term> lexical retrieval to ground each classification in similar
        documents. I expected a clear win. The measured lift was +1.9% category
        accuracy, a net of one correct call in 54. On domain the lift was +0.0%:
        grounding fixed three calls and broke three. Lexical retrieval does not
        earn the cost of a move to embeddings here.</p>
      <p>The comparison itself had a bug. I scored the <em>new-prompt</em>
        grounded arm against an <em>old-prompt</em> frozen baseline, and that
        inflated the lift. I rebuilt the baseline so both arms ran the same
        prompt, and the advantage disappeared. A three-pass confirm settled it.
        Across 162 grounded classifications, grounding fixed a domain call
        <strong>zero</strong> times and broke four.</p>
      <p>So I retired it
        (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/archive/012-retire-bm25-grounding.md">ADR-012</a>).
        It came out of the shipped path and out of the CI gate. The retrieval
        code, the corpus, and the measurement scripts stay in the repo, dormant
        and reproducible. The verdict is that BM25 does not justify itself.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The lesson:</strong> the first measurement said
        skip embeddings. A fair baseline said cut BM25. The comparison only
        holds if exactly one thing changes.</p>
    </div>


    <div class="toc-return"><a href="#toc-heading">Contents</a></div>
    <h2 id="why-an-llm-at-all">Why an LLM at all</h2>
    <p>
      Does this task need an LLM? I built the boring baseline: TF-IDF into
      logistic regression, standard and untuned. I scored it against the same 54
      hand-labeled rows as everything else on this page.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The LLM won by 20 points on category and 26 on domain</strong>
      (paired exact McNemar, p=0.013 and p=0.0005). The baseline collapses toward
      whichever class is lexically loud. The spend is therefore for the
      boundaries that need reading comprehension.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two handicaps cut against the baseline. Its training labels came from a
      judge that disagrees with humans 5&ndash;6% of the time.
      <code>industry</code> also had exactly one training row. A margin this size
      survives them.
      <a class="card-link" href="classifier-baseline.html">The full bake-off, handicaps included <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
    </p>
    <p>
      The baseline runs with no key and no server. It is vendored into this site
      and classifies live in your tab. Pick a row it gets wrong, and watch which
      class it picks instead.
      <a class="card-link" href="classifier-baseline.html#try-it">Run the losing baseline <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
    </p>

    <div class="toc-return"><a href="#toc-heading">Contents</a></div>
    <h2 id="two-ways-of-not-fooling-myself">Scale and routing</h2>
    <p>
      Fifty-four hand-labeled snippets is a small set. The 95% interval on that
      gold is about 13 points wide. Two follow-ups sit on that.
    </p>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">The scaled eval</p>
      <p>A couple of lucky rows can look like progress on n=54. So I scaled to
        <strong>300</strong> real snippets, and the Opus judge graded them. That
        roughly halves each interval: category to <strong>93.3%</strong>, domain
        to <strong>90.3%</strong>. Those corroborate the hand-graded
        numbers.</p>

