    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>The upgrade that bought nothing</p>
      <p>The roadmap called for tiered routing: escalate the uncertain cases to
        the more expensive model. I built the trigger, measured it, and shipped
        the negative result. Routing moved <strong>+0 rows</strong> on both axes
        at about <strong>1.97&times;</strong> the cost per article. Of nine
        escalations on the gold set it fixed zero and broke one. I sent
        <em>everything</em> to the premium model, and it scored the same 94.4%
        category. So no router had any headroom.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why it shipped as a no:</strong> the earlier
        prompt fix already took the ground routing was aimed at. One more finding
        stayed in the record: a new field in the schema flipped one benign
        snippet into a model refusal. The measurement apparatus changed the
        behavior it measured. The full verdict is in
        <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/archive/013-decline-tiered-routing.md">ADR-013</a>.
        A separate project measured
        <a href="faithfulness-judge.html">how reliable an LLM judge is</a>, and
        it landed on the same verdict. Three independent measurements now state
        that escalation barely pays.</p>
    </div>


