    <p>
      v3 added <code>region</code>, and it breaks the output contract on purpose.
      Callers that expected two fields now get three. There are six labels, and
      <code>global</code> is the single catch-all. It takes stories with no
      geographic anchor, and stories that span several. A split of &ldquo;no
      geography&rdquo; from &ldquo;worldwide&rdquo; would have put the fuzziest
      boundary on the most common kind of story in the corpus. I wrote down the
      label set, the boundary rules, and the scope limits before the build in
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/014-region-field-design.md">ADR-014</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Region landed at 87.0% against fresh hand labels when the axis shipped.
      <strong>All seven</strong> first-pass misses were stories whose true label
      is <code>global</code>. The model pulled each one to a specific region,
      usually <code>americas</code>. The rule is to label the theater the
      activity happens in, and only when the text names it. The model repeatedly
      saw a US actor and inferred a US theater.
    </p>
    <p>
      Region now reads
      <strong><span data-metric="region_accuracy">94.4%</span></strong>. The fix
      is one bullet of prompt. It tells the model that a named US command or
      contractor identifies the actor, not a place. The identical clause shipped
      in two rounds, and the second round used a ruler twice the size. The first
      experiment did not have the power to decide.
    </p>

